WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.200 --> 00:00:02.633
- Conetoe was a food desert.

00:00:04.500 --> 00:00:07.180
But I wanted to do somethin' about it.

00:00:07.180 --> 00:00:09.770
We began to grow, and we began

00:00:09.770 --> 00:00:12.130
to put our hearts and hands together,

00:00:12.130 --> 00:00:15.100
and Conetoe is no longer a food desert.

00:00:15.100 --> 00:00:17.219
Conetoe is a food oasis.

00:00:17.219 --> 00:00:19.719
(piano music)

00:00:27.300 --> 00:00:28.947
I'm Reverend Richard R. Joyner,

00:00:28.947 --> 00:00:32.270
founder of Conetoe Family Life Center.

00:00:32.270 --> 00:00:33.930
Conetoe is a rural area

00:00:33.930 --> 00:00:35.630
in the eastern part of North Carolina.

00:00:35.630 --> 00:00:38.500
Population of about 300 people.

00:00:38.500 --> 00:00:41.780
A food desert really means
that the residents here

00:00:41.780 --> 00:00:46.780
do not have access to fresh,
locally affordable grown foods.

00:00:46.850 --> 00:00:49.560
We would buy most of our
food from discount stores,

00:00:49.560 --> 00:00:51.520
which are high in sodium, calories,

00:00:51.520 --> 00:00:53.763
very low in nutritional values.

00:00:54.660 --> 00:00:58.090
People had these early
stages of chronic disease,

00:00:58.090 --> 00:01:02.730
diabetes, high blood pressure,
heart attacks, strokes,

00:01:02.730 --> 00:01:07.720
and even mental illness because
of the stress and the pain.

00:01:07.720 --> 00:01:10.450
People were dyin' in their 30s and 40s.

00:01:10.450 --> 00:01:13.740
We would be havin' two
to three funerals a month

00:01:13.740 --> 00:01:15.923
and really devastating families.

00:01:18.783 --> 00:01:19.960
(engine rumbling)

00:01:19.960 --> 00:01:22.840
I was really feelin' overwhelmed,
and feelin' hopeless,

00:01:22.840 --> 00:01:26.690
because I couldn't stop
this chronic disease.

00:01:26.690 --> 00:01:27.970
I pulled on the side of the road

00:01:27.970 --> 00:01:30.030
and really started praying,

00:01:30.030 --> 00:01:31.733
wanting to know what could I do.

00:01:32.680 --> 00:01:36.340
And I had this epiphany of
open your eyes, look around.

00:01:36.340 --> 00:01:39.658
And I'd open my eyes,
and all I saw was fields.

00:01:39.658 --> 00:01:42.158
(piano music)

00:01:43.150 --> 00:01:46.950
So, we started our first small garden.

00:01:46.950 --> 00:01:49.693
And now we're up to 21 acres of farmland.

00:01:50.680 --> 00:01:52.470
We have about eight people

00:01:52.470 --> 00:01:55.230
that are parents and
grandparents of our youth,

00:01:55.230 --> 00:01:57.270
that work here every day.

00:01:57.270 --> 00:02:01.940
We have 40 students durin'
the summer that use this farm

00:02:01.940 --> 00:02:04.140
for their readin',
writin', math and science

00:02:04.140 --> 00:02:05.513
and for food.

00:02:07.550 --> 00:02:11.690
Presently on our farm, we
have blueberries, tomatoes;

00:02:11.690 --> 00:02:15.120
we have collards; we have
squash; we have zucchini.

00:02:15.120 --> 00:02:19.170
We end up averaging 100
pounds of food per week

00:02:19.170 --> 00:02:20.310
out of the garden.

00:02:20.310 --> 00:02:23.230
Food that we grow has
had a tangible impact

00:02:23.230 --> 00:02:24.780
upon our community.

00:02:24.780 --> 00:02:27.769
We're able to put food on people's tables.

00:02:27.769 --> 00:02:29.620
Hello, I have your vegetables.
- Hello, Reverend Joyner.

00:02:29.620 --> 00:02:34.070
- [Richard] All right, our
community is spending $4,000

00:02:34.070 --> 00:02:38.647
less a month on prescription
pills, because the food--

00:02:40.940 --> 00:02:42.450
- The deaths have slowed down;

00:02:42.450 --> 00:02:44.510
we're not havin' as many funerals.

00:02:44.510 --> 00:02:47.400
I feel like we are makin' a difference.

00:02:47.400 --> 00:02:51.590
I love this community
here because we're unitin'

00:02:51.590 --> 00:02:54.835
around savin' lives together.

00:02:54.835 --> 00:02:58.168
(acoustic guitar music)

00:03:01.505 --> 00:03:04.505
(electronic music)

